<chapter xml:id="ch-study-design">
  <title>Study Design</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A working pipeline demonstrates that the analytic framework can be implemented.
      It does not establish that the implementation produces dependable research data
      or improves the coding process. That claim requires an empirical study comparing
      the pipeline with independently developed human coding on real student responses.
    </p>

    <p>
      The proposed study evaluates three coding conditions: a human-only workflow, a
      deterministic computational baseline, and an LLM-assisted pipeline. The study
      examines more than final-code accuracy. It also measures human agreement,
      researcher time, review volume, reviewer overrides, trace completeness, and the
      types of disagreement each workflow reveals.
    </p>

    <p>
      The design treats uncertainty and disagreement as data. A response may expose an
      ambiguous episode boundary, an incomplete inference, overlapping codebook
      definitions, missing vocabulary, or an unstable coding rule. The study therefore
      asks not only whether the pipeline agrees with an accepted human result, but
      also whether it helps researchers locate and understand the source of a
      disagreement.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-study-contribution">
    <title>Proposed Contribution</title>

    <p>
      The project makes a methods contribution only if it delivers three connected
      results: a defensible analytic framing, an auditable workflow, and empirical
      evidence about how that workflow performs. None of the three is sufficient by
      itself.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-framing-contribution">
      <title>Analytic Framing</title>

      <p>
        The first contribution is a response-level operationalization of a broader
        theoretical perspective on mathematical control structures. The project uses
        the Episode Model to represent the local justification visible in one written
        response. It does not treat one response as a complete student conception.
      </p>

      <p>
        This framing makes several analytic distinctions explicit. It separates the
        criterion a student cites, the inferential link the student uses, and the
        judgment the student draws. It also separates local reasoning quality from
        final-answer correctness and from coverage of the full question goal. These
        distinctions allow disagreements to be located more precisely than a single
        response-level label would permit.
      </p>

      <p>
        The study does not revalidate the theoretical framework as a whole. It tests
        whether this response-level operationalization can be applied consistently and
        whether it supports useful comparison among human and computational coding
        workflows. The theoretical basis appears in
        <xref ref="ch-theoretical-foundation" text="title"/>, and the analytic unit is
        defined in <xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-method-contribution">
      <title>Workflow Contribution</title>

      <p>
        The second contribution is an auditable human-in-the-loop workflow. Rather
        than producing only a final code, the pipeline records the response source,
        screening decision, proposed episode structure, candidate code matches,
        confidence or uncertainty, review disposition, and any later human override.
        Each run also records the governing codebook, vocabulary, protocol, prompt,
        model, and software versions.
      </p>

      <p>
        This structure supports two forms of review. A researcher can inspect one case
        from the original response through the final disposition, and the research team
        can aggregate recurring disagreements across cases. The latter may reveal a
        weak extraction rule, an overlapping code definition, missing vocabulary, or a
        coding policy that human researchers are applying inconsistently.
      </p>

      <p>
        The workflow contribution is therefore not automation alone. It is the
        conversion of an interpretive coding process into explicit stages whose outputs,
        rationales, and revisions can be inspected.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-empirical-contribution">
      <title>Empirical Contribution</title>

      <p>
        The third contribution is the evaluation itself. The study must determine
        whether the pipeline improves or clarifies the research process relative to the
        human-only workflow. The relevant outcomes include:
        <ul>
          <li>agreement with accepted human coding;</li>
          <li>consistency across independent coders and repeated runs;</li>
          <li>time required to code and review responses;</li>
          <li>the amount and distribution of human review;</li>
          <li>the completeness of the audit trail;</li>
          <li>the rate and type of reviewer overrides; and</li>
          <li>
            the ability to diagnose codebook, vocabulary, protocol, and tool failures.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A software demonstration, a small set of illustrative examples, or a high
        aggregate accuracy value would not establish this contribution. The study needs
        a real research corpus, independent human coding, a held-out evaluation set,
        pre-specified comparisons, and qualitative analysis of representative errors.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-study-corpus">
    <title>Corpus and Sampling</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-data-sources">
      <title>Data Sources</title>

      <p>
        The research corpus consists of real short-answer responses to mathematical
        reading questions delivered through interactive textbooks. Each response must
        remain linked to the question it answered and to the information needed to
        interpret that question. At minimum, the research record should preserve:
        <ul>
          <li>a stable, de-identified response identifier;</li>
          <li>the exact response text;</li>
          <li>the question identifier and prompt;</li>
          <li>the course and offering from which the response came;</li>
          <li>the relevant mathematical reference or expected reasoning;</li>
          <li>the source and ingestion version; and</li>
          <li>the inclusion boundary used to form the corpus.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Repository fixtures and synthetic examples may be used to test ingestion,
        train coders, or illustrate the method. They are not research evidence and
        must not be included in the validation results. The final report should state
        clearly which records are authentic student responses and which were created
        for development or training.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before sampling begins, the research team should create a corpus inventory
        listing the participating courses, semesters, questions, response counts, and
        any exclusions. The current source material establishes that the corpus will
        span several questions, but it does not yet fix the final courses, semesters,
        question families, or sample size.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-sampling">
      <title>Sampling and Stratification</title>

      <p>
        A simple random sample could contain mostly easy or repetitive cases and would
        provide little evidence about the situations in which the pipeline is intended
        to assist. The evaluation sample should therefore be stratified by question and
        by expected coding difficulty.
      </p>

      <p>
        At minimum, the sample should represent:
        <ul>
          <li>clear single-episode or single-control responses;</li>
          <li>blank, no-effort, and restatement responses;</li>
          <li>bare answers with no justification;</li>
          <li>incomplete episodes;</li>
          <li>responses containing several possible control structures;</li>
          <li>chained, independent, or nested reasoning;</li>
          <li>multiple-match cases;</li>
          <li>out-of-vocabulary and no-match cases;</li>
          <li>
            responses with a true criterion but an incomplete or invalid inferential
            link; and
          </li>
          <li>
            responses with sound local reasoning but incomplete coverage of the
            question goal.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The corpus should be divided into non-overlapping sets serving distinct
        purposes:
        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>Onboarding set</title>
            <p>
              Synthetic or non-evaluation responses used to teach the framework and
              coding workflow.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Calibration set</title>
            <p>
              A shared set coded independently before formal work begins and used to
              identify unclear rules and assess coder readiness.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Development set</title>
            <p>
              Responses used to refine deterministic rules, prompts, vocabulary, and
              codebook patterns. Results on this set are not reported as held-out
              performance.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Validation set</title>
            <p>
              A held-out set used only after the governing materials and comparison
              conditions have been frozen.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The development roadmap recommends at least 100 already-coded responses,
        stratified by the screening label, for the initial Stage 2 screening study.
        That recommendation does not determine the final sample size for the full
        multi-stage validation. The research team must select and justify the final
        sizes before evaluation.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-ethics">
      <title>Ethics, Consent, and De-identification</title>

      <p>
        The final study report must state the ethics and consent basis for using the
        student responses. The corpus should contain only the information required for
        the analysis, and published examples should be masked or paraphrased when
        necessary to prevent identification.
      </p>

      <p>
        LLM-assisted analysis introduces a separate data-governance question. Before
        any response text is sent to an external service, the research team must confirm
        that the intended transmission, provider, storage policy, and retention policy
        are permitted by the applicable IRB determination and institutional rules.
        De-identifying a response does not by itself establish that external processing
        is authorized.
      </p>

      <p>
        The study record should document:
        <ul>
          <li>the IRB protocol, exemption, or other formal determination;</li>
          <li>the consent basis and permitted research uses;</li>
          <li>the de-identification procedure;</li>
          <li>who may access identifiable and de-identified data;</li>
          <li>where data and review records are stored;</li>
          <li>whether an external model provider receives response text; and</li>
          <li>restrictions on quoting or publishing student responses.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <aside xml:id="aside-study-open-specifications">
        <title>Specifications Still to Be Finalized</title>
        <p>
          The source material does not yet establish the following study details. They
          should be entered here before the chapter is treated as the final protocol.
        </p>
        <table xml:id="tab-study-open-specifications">
          <title>Required study decisions.</title>
          <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
            <col width="32%"/>
            <col width="68%"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
              <cell>Decision</cell>
              <cell>Required record</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Corpus scope</cell>
              <cell>Courses, semesters, questions, and inclusion dates.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Sample sizes</cell>
              <cell>Counts for calibration, development, and validation sets.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>IRB and governance</cell>
              <cell>Protocol or determination, API permissions, and access controls.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Coder readiness</cell>
              <cell>Evidence or threshold required before independent research coding.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Frozen versions</cell>
              <cell>Protocol, codebook, vocabulary, prompt, model, and software versions.</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Primary metrics</cell>
              <cell>Exact agreement statistics and treatment of multi-label codes.</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </aside>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-study-gold-standard">
    <title>Human Coding and the Gold Standard</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-independent-coding">
      <title>Independent Coding</title>

      <p>
        At least two trained coders should analyze the same validation responses
        independently. Their first-pass decisions must be made without seeing the
        pipeline's candidate codes, confidence values, or dispositions. This blind
        first pass protects the human comparison from anchoring on the tool's proposal.
      </p>

      <p>
        The independent record should preserve the decisions needed to evaluate the
        full workflow, including:
        <ul>
          <li>screening decision and rationale;</li>
          <li>number and boundaries of episodes;</li>
          <li>criterion, inferential link, and judgment for each episode;</li>
          <li>relationships among episodes;</li>
          <li>candidate control-structure codes;</li>
          <li>criterion and inferential-link correctness, when eligible;</li>
          <li>goal coverage;</li>
          <li>OOV, no-match, ambiguity, or policy flags;</li>
          <li>confidence and uncertainty notes; and</li>
          <li>recommended disposition.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Coders should use the same frozen versions of the question reference, coding
        protocol, codebook, and controlled vocabulary. They should record coding time
        and work in bounded sittings so that efficiency and fatigue can be examined
        rather than left as unobserved influences.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-calibration">
      <title>Calibration</title>

      <p>
        Calibration precedes formal validation. Coders first analyze a shared set
        independently, compare their decisions, identify the earliest point of each
        disagreement, and <xref ref="g-adjudication" text="custom">adjudicate</xref>
        the cases using the original question, response, and current coding materials.
      </p>

      <p>
        The calibration set should contain the same broad range of response types as
        the validation set, including incomplete, multi-episode, multiple-match, OOV,
        invalid-link, and partial-coverage cases. When a disagreement reveals an unclear
        definition or policy, the research team should revise and test the relevant
        material before freezing the study versions.
      </p>

      <p>
        The calibration record should preserve both coders' original decisions, the
        evidence supporting each interpretation, the disagreement category, the
        adjudicated result or unresolved status, and any follow-up change. Calibration
        responses must not later be counted as held-out validation cases.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-adjudication">
      <title>Adjudication</title>

      <p>
        Adjudication begins only after the independent decisions have been preserved
        and pre-adjudication agreement has been calculated. The purpose is not to erase
        disagreement but to produce a documented research decision when the available
        evidence and coding rules support one.
      </p>

      <p>
        For each disagreement, the adjudicators should:
        <ol>
          <li>return to the exact question and response;</li>
          <li>identify the earliest workflow stage at which the codings diverged;</li>
          <li>state the evidence for each interpretation;</li>
          <li>consult the frozen question reference, protocol, and codebook;</li>
          <li>record the agreed result when one interpretation is defensible;</li>
          <li>preserve the case as unresolved when the evidence is insufficient; and</li>
          <li>
            record whether the disagreement suggests a future change to the coding
            materials.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        The resulting adjudicated annotations form the gold-standard comparison set.
        They should not be presented as infallible labels. They are the documented
        outcome of the study's human coding and adjudication procedure under specified
        versions of the governing materials.
      </p>

      <p>
        If adjudication exposes a flaw serious enough to require an immediate codebook
        or policy change, the team should version the change and decide whether the
        affected portion of the validation study must be restarted. It should not
        silently reinterpret earlier records under a new rule.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-human-ceiling">
      <title>Human Agreement as a Reference Point</title>

      <p>
        Human–human agreement should be reported before adjudication. At minimum, the
        report should include percent agreement and either Cohen's <m>\kappa</m> or
        Krippendorff's <m>\alpha</m>, chosen to match the number of coders, missing-data
        pattern, and form of the coding decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        Agreement should be examined separately at major decision levels rather than
        collapsed immediately into one statistic. Relevant levels include screening,
        episode count or boundaries, component interpretation, code assignment,
        correctness, goal coverage, and final disposition.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pre-adjudication human agreement is an empirical reference point for judging
        the difficulty and stability of the task. It is sometimes described as a human
        ceiling, but it is not a strict numerical upper bound on agreement with the
        adjudicated labels. A system may agree with an adjudicated result more often
        than either individual coder did while still failing in important ways. Human
        disagreement must therefore be interpreted alongside system performance and the
        error analysis.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-study-comparisons">
    <title>Comparison Conditions</title>

    <p>
      The study compares three workflows on the same held-out responses and under the
      same frozen analytic materials. The comparison is intended to separate the value
      of the staged architecture from the additional value, if any, of LLM-assisted
      language interpretation.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-human-only">
      <title>Human-Only Workflow</title>

      <p>
        In the human-only condition, trained coders apply the protocol and codebook
        using the ordinary spreadsheet or manual workflow. They do not receive
        pipeline-generated episodes, candidate codes, confidence values, or review
        dispositions during the independent pass.
      </p>

      <p>
        The study should record coding and adjudication time, uncertainty notes, and
        disagreements under this condition. These records establish the current labor
        requirements and the difficulties that exist without computational assistance.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-deterministic">
      <title>Deterministic Baseline</title>

      <p>
        The deterministic condition runs the rule-based pipeline without an LLM. In
        the current prototype, this includes a rule-based eligibility screener, a
        connector- and pattern-based extractor, a pattern matcher, and a rule-based
        disposer. The pipeline returns structured proposals and review dispositions
        using fixed rules.
      </p>

      <p>
        This condition is load-bearing. It shows how much performance and auditability
        come from explicit pipeline structure alone. Without it, any difference between
        the human-only and LLM-assisted conditions could not be attributed specifically
        to the language model.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-llm-assisted">
      <title>LLM-Assisted Workflow</title>

      <p>
        The LLM-assisted condition uses the same pipeline architecture and output
        schemas as the deterministic condition. Language-model components replace only
        selected language-heavy stages. The current roadmap places LLM assistance in
        screening and episode extraction while retaining deterministic ingestion,
        codebook matching, and disposition logic.
      </p>

      <p>
        The language model produces structured, reviewable proposals rather than final
        research codes. The study must record the model identifier, prompt version,
        decoding or sampling settings, vocabulary version, codebook version, and any
        retries or failures. A material change to these settings creates a new study
        condition rather than an invisible update to the old one.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project treats the LLM as unreliable at the mathematical judgments needed
        for criterion truth, inferential validity, and final adjudication. Those
        decisions remain under human authority even when the model assists with
        screening or extraction.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-ablation">
      <title>Ablation Studies</title>

      <p>
        The primary planned ablation compares two extraction policies:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Literal extraction</title>
            <p>
              Record only reasoning components that are clearly present in the
              student's words or notation.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Interpretive extraction</title>
            <p>
              Permit the extractor to recover implicit links or normalize the
              student's language when the intended relation appears sufficiently
              supported.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        This comparison tests a central methodological tradeoff. Literal extraction
        may omit recoverable reasoning, whereas interpretive extraction may attribute
        reasoning the student did not express. The study should compare their agreement,
        review burden, override rate, and characteristic errors.
      </p>

      <p>
        Additional ablations may be added only if they are specified before the held-out
        validation set is examined. Exploratory comparisons discovered after evaluation
        should be labeled exploratory rather than reported as pre-planned confirmatory
        results.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-study-outcomes">
    <title>Outcomes and Analysis</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-reliability-outcomes">
      <title>Reliability</title>

      <p>
        Reliability outcomes should distinguish human agreement from agreement between
        a computational workflow and the adjudicated human set.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-study-reliability-measures">
        <title>Primary reliability comparisons.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="30%"/>
          <col width="34%"/>
          <col width="36%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Decision</cell>
            <cell>Comparison</cell>
            <cell>Planned summary</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Screening</cell>
            <cell>Coder–coder and workflow–gold standard</cell>
            <cell>Agreement, accuracy, precision, recall, and <m>F_1</m>.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Control-structure assignment</cell>
            <cell>Coder–coder and workflow–gold standard</cell>
            <cell>Code-level agreement and a pre-specified multi-label summary.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Episode structure</cell>
            <cell>Coder–coder and workflow–gold standard</cell>
            <cell>Episode-count, boundary, and component disagreements.</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Disposition</cell>
            <cell>Workflow distribution and human review result</cell>
            <cell>Counts, percentages, confirmations, and overrides.</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Because code assignment may be multi-label and episode structures may not align
        one-to-one, the exact scoring rule must be chosen before evaluation. The final
        protocol should state how multiple codes, unresolved cases, and unmatched
        episodes enter each statistic.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reliability results should be reported overall and by question, response type,
        and difficulty stratum when the sample size permits. Aggregate performance may
        conceal that a workflow handles clear single-episode responses well while
        failing systematically on OOV, nested, or invalid-link cases.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-efficiency-outcomes">
      <title>Efficiency</title>

      <p>
        Efficiency concerns the amount and organization of human labor, not merely
        computational runtime. The study should record:
        <ul>
          <li>total and per-response coding time;</li>
          <li>time required for review and adjudication;</li>
          <li>the number and percentage of responses auto-accepted;</li>
          <li>the number routed to standard review or escalation;</li>
          <li>the number flagged for terminology or no-match review;</li>
          <li>the number of candidate codes inspected per response;</li>
          <li>the rate of confirmation and each type of override; and</li>
          <li>the amount of recoding caused by later policy or codebook changes.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A reduction in first-pass coding time is not automatically beneficial. It may
        be offset by increased review, poorly targeted escalations, or correction of
        confident but unsupported proposals. The study should therefore compare the
        full path from initial coding through final documented disposition.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-audit-outcomes">
      <title>Auditability</title>

      <p>
        Auditability measures whether a result can be traced and whether disagreement
        can be localized. For each pipeline run, the expected trace includes:
        <ul>
          <li>the source response and question;</li>
          <li>the output and rationale from each stage;</li>
          <li>the governing analytic and software versions;</li>
          <li>the candidate codes and confidence information;</li>
          <li>the assigned review disposition;</li>
          <li>the human decision and evidence-based rationale; and</li>
          <li>the complete sequence of confirmations or overrides.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The validation report should state how often the required trace is complete and
        how often a disagreement can be assigned to a specific stage or policy. It
        should also report how often review identifies a needed vocabulary addition,
        codebook clarification, protocol revision, or software correction.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-error-taxonomy">
      <title>Error and Disagreement Taxonomy</title>

      <p>
        Quantitative summaries should be paired with an error taxonomy. Each
        workflow–human disagreement should be assigned to the earliest applicable
        category:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening error</title>
            <p>
              A response is included, excluded, or flagged differently from the
              adjudicated decision.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Episode-boundary error</title>
            <p>
              The workflow identifies the wrong number of episodes or places their
              boundaries differently.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Component-interpretation error</title>
            <p>
              The criterion, inferential link, or judgment is missing, added, or
              interpreted incorrectly.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Relation error</title>
            <p>
              Chained, independent, or nested relationships are represented
              incorrectly.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Matching error</title>
            <p>
              The extracted episode is compared with the codebook incorrectly or is
              assigned an unsupported code.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Vocabulary or OOV error</title>
            <p>
              Relevant language is missed, normalized incorrectly, or forced into an
              existing term or code.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Correctness or coverage error</title>
            <p>
              Criterion truth, inferential validity, or question-goal coverage is
              evaluated incorrectly.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Policy error</title>
            <p>
              The governing coding convention is unclear, incomplete, or applied
              inconsistently.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Human-instability case</title>
            <p>
              Independent coders or adjudicators apply the same materials differently
              in a way not attributable solely to the computational workflow.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The report should provide counts and representative cases for each category.
        The examples should preserve the distinction between a tool failure, a genuine
        ambiguity in the response, and a weakness in the human coding system. Reporting
        only aggregate performance would hide the methodological information the
        staged workflow is designed to reveal.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-study-limitations">
    <title>Scope, Threats, and Limitations</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-generality">
      <title>Generality</title>

      <p>
        The initial validation is a bounded case study using a limited set of questions,
        mathematical topics, courses, and institutional settings. Its results may show
        that the method is workable and useful in those settings; they do not establish
        universal performance across mathematical domains or student populations.
      </p>

      <p>
        Question-relative reasoning is central to the method. A code or inferential
        link that is valid for one question may not be valid for another. Claims of
        transfer should therefore be based on explicit replication across new questions
        and topics rather than assumed from aggregate success within the initial corpus.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-moving-targets">
      <title>Moving Codebooks and Protocols</title>

      <p>
        Tool performance cannot be interpreted against an unstable target. The
        question reference, coding protocol, codebook, controlled vocabulary, nesting
        policy, prompt, model, and software configuration must be frozen before the
        held-out evaluation begins.
      </p>

      <p>
        If a serious flaw requires a change during validation, the change should receive
        a new version and its effect should be documented. The team must then decide
        whether to restart the affected comparison, report separate pre-change and
        post-change results, or classify the finding as exploratory. Earlier records
        should never be silently recoded under a revised rule.
      </p>

      <p>
        Anchoring and learning effects are related threats. Human coders must complete
        their independent pass before seeing tool proposals, and the held-out set should
        not be reused repeatedly for prompt or rule development. Otherwise, apparent
        improvement may reflect adaptation to the evaluation set rather than a more
        dependable method.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-study-overclaiming">
      <title>Interpretive Overreach</title>

      <p>
        The study evaluates coding of evidence visible in short written responses. It
        does not validate claims that the pipeline detects complete conceptions,
        reconstructs everything a student believed, or predicts how the student would
        reason in other settings.
      </p>

      <p>
        The pipeline should propose candidate analyses and review dispositions, not
        final research claims. It must not complete a missing judgment for the student,
        treat a correct final answer as proof of valid reasoning, or force ambiguous and
        OOV cases into an existing code merely to improve aggregate agreement.
      </p>

      <p>
        The LLM-assisted condition creates an additional risk of plausible but
        unsupported interpretation. Preserving literal response evidence, comparing
        against a deterministic baseline, and requiring human adjudication are therefore
        essential features of the method rather than optional safeguards.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      The validation study converts the project from a framework and software design
      into an empirical methods investigation. Independent human coding establishes the
      stability and difficulty of the task; adjudication produces a documented comparison
      set; the human-only, deterministic, and LLM-assisted conditions separate different
      sources of value; and the outcome analyses test reliability, efficiency,
      auditability, and disagreement diagnosis together.
    </p>

    <p>
      The study succeeds only if its conclusions remain proportional to the evidence.
      A favorable result would show that the pipeline improves or clarifies coding within
      a specified corpus and under frozen analytic rules. It would not eliminate human
      judgment or establish unrestricted generality. Instead, it would provide a
      reproducible basis for refining the method and testing it in additional questions,
      topics, and settings.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>
